1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:01,212
You are the reason the

2
00:00:01,212 --> 00:00:02,426
sloop is still floating.

3
00:00:02,426 --> 00:00:03,542
Nobody has thanked you

4
00:00:03,542 --> 00:00:04,909
for it in four episodes.

5
00:00:04,909 --> 00:00:07,313
They have been busy with coats,

6
00:00:07,313 --> 00:00:08,861
votes and medicine chests,

7
00:00:08,861 --> 00:00:12,198
while you were down in the hold with a hammer,

8
00:00:12,198 --> 00:00:13,869
keeping the sea outside.

9
00:00:13,869 --> 00:00:15,497
So far in this series,

10
00:00:15,497 --> 00:00:16,817
you have been Tom,

11
00:00:16,817 --> 00:00:20,523
the captain, the quartermaster and the surgeon the crew kept.

12
00:00:21,201 --> 00:00:23,267
This time, you are the man who was

13
00:00:23,267 --> 00:00:25,751
below the whole time: the carpenter.

14
00:00:26,334 --> 00:00:29,234
Imagine you are a ship's carpenter of about fifty,

15
00:00:29,625 --> 00:00:31,315
aboard the same modest

16
00:00:31,315 --> 00:00:33,728
Caribbean sloop around seventeen seventeen.

17
00:00:34,281 --> 00:00:35,563
You are broad and bald,

18
00:00:35,563 --> 00:00:37,236
with a short gray beard,

19
00:00:37,236 --> 00:00:40,875
rolled sleeves and a brown canvas apron full of tools.

20
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:43,751
You joined this crew of your own free will,

21
00:00:43,751 --> 00:00:45,572
long before Tom arrived.

22
00:00:45,572 --> 00:00:45,969
You,

23
00:00:45,969 --> 00:00:49,500
this crew and this voyage are imagined.

24
00:00:49,500 --> 00:00:51,679
The problems come from trial records,

25
00:00:51,679 --> 00:00:53,151
eyewitness accounts and

26
00:00:53,151 --> 00:00:55,784
historians' research on real pirate crews.

27
00:00:55,784 --> 00:00:58,019
And remember what the job was.

28
00:00:58,019 --> 00:00:59,167
Every repair you make

29
00:00:59,167 --> 00:01:00,917
keeps a crew of robbers at sea.

30
00:01:00,917 --> 00:01:02,677
The sailors on the ships they

31
00:01:02,677 --> 00:01:04,326
board are not scenery,

32
00:01:04,764 --> 00:01:07,783
even when our story keeps the violence off screen.

33
00:01:07,783 --> 00:01:10,009
Your day starts at the pump.

34
00:01:10,009 --> 00:01:12,883
A wooden hull always lets some water in,

35
00:01:12,883 --> 00:01:15,466
and yours lets in more than most.

36
00:01:15,466 --> 00:01:17,395
Half the water Tom carried

37
00:01:17,395 --> 00:01:19,035
in his famous bucket was,

38
00:01:19,035 --> 00:01:21,979
strictly speaking, your department.

39
00:01:21,979 --> 00:01:24,983
You sound the well with a weighted line,

40
00:01:24,983 --> 00:01:26,271
read the wet mark,

41
00:01:26,271 --> 00:01:28,305
and decide how worried to be.

42
00:01:28,305 --> 00:01:30,537
Then you go looking for the

43
00:01:30,537 --> 00:01:32,368
leak with a lantern, a mallet,

44
00:01:32,368 --> 00:01:35,124
a bag of oakum and a pot of hot pitch.

45
00:01:35,124 --> 00:01:37,311
Oakum is old rope picked

46
00:01:37,311 --> 00:01:38,635
apart into loose fibers.

47
00:01:38,635 --> 00:01:41,745
You hammer it into the seams between the planks,

48
00:01:41,745 --> 00:01:42,839
seal it with pitch,

49
00:01:42,839 --> 00:01:45,810
and the sea comes in a little more slowly.

50
00:01:45,810 --> 00:01:47,060
That is the whole

51
00:01:47,060 --> 00:01:49,282
glamorous secret of your trade.

52
00:01:49,282 --> 00:01:51,770
You know why you left merchant ships.

53
00:01:51,770 --> 00:01:54,365
The economist Peter Leeson quotes

54
00:01:54,365 --> 00:01:56,486
the maritime historian Ralph Davis:

55
00:01:56,486 --> 00:01:58,076
on a merchant ship,

56
00:01:58,076 --> 00:02:01,261
the captain "had absolute authority over the mates,

57
00:02:01,261 --> 00:02:04,435
the carpenters and boatswain, and the seamen."

58
00:02:04,954 --> 00:02:05,617
On this sloop,

59
00:02:05,617 --> 00:02:07,952
the captain cannot even keep his own coat.

60
00:02:08,487 --> 00:02:09,645
Buccaneer crews knew

61
00:02:09,645 --> 00:02:11,189
what carpenters were worth.

62
00:02:11,189 --> 00:02:12,756
Alexander Exquemelin,

63
00:02:12,756 --> 00:02:15,658
who sailed with them in the sixteen sixties and seventies,

64
00:02:16,109 --> 00:02:18,365
wrote that before any prize was shared,

65
00:02:18,365 --> 00:02:20,282
the carpenter who careened,

66
00:02:20,282 --> 00:02:23,884
mended and rigged the vessel received one hundred

67
00:02:23,884 --> 00:02:25,544
or one hundred and fifty pieces of eight.

68
00:02:25,544 --> 00:02:26,791
That sounds generous

69
00:02:26,791 --> 00:02:29,185
until you remember the surgeon.

70
00:02:29,185 --> 00:02:30,893
His medicine chest alone was

71
00:02:30,893 --> 00:02:32,738
rated at two hundred or more.

72
00:02:32,738 --> 00:02:34,890
As the surgeon heard last time,

73
00:02:34,890 --> 00:02:36,790
a box of jars was rated above

74
00:02:36,790 --> 00:02:38,641
the man who keeps the ship afloat.

75
00:02:38,641 --> 00:02:39,875
You noticed.

76
00:02:39,875 --> 00:02:40,910
You said nothing.

77
00:02:40,910 --> 00:02:42,117
You are a carpenter.

78
00:02:42,117 --> 00:02:43,510
You measure twice.

79
00:02:43,883 --> 00:02:46,103
Pirates valued carpenters so highly

80
00:02:46,103 --> 00:02:49,685
that many never got the chance to volunteer.

81
00:02:49,685 --> 00:02:51,499
In December seventeen sixteen,

82
00:02:51,499 --> 00:02:54,001
two pirate sloops under Samuel Bellamy

83
00:02:54,001 --> 00:02:56,630
and Louis Lebous took the Bristol

84
00:02:56,630 --> 00:02:58,552
ship Saint Michael, bound for Jamaica.

85
00:02:58,552 --> 00:03:00,693
According to the depositions in

86
00:03:00,693 --> 00:03:01,907
the Boston court files,

87
00:03:01,907 --> 00:03:04,322
they kept her carpenter, Thomas Davis,

88
00:03:04,322 --> 00:03:07,239
because he was "a carpenter and a single man."

89
00:03:07,693 --> 00:03:09,337
Davis begged to be released.

90
00:03:09,337 --> 00:03:10,925
Bellamy promised to let him

91
00:03:10,925 --> 00:03:12,920
go when they took the next ship.

92
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:14,164
When the moment came,

93
00:03:14,164 --> 00:03:15,802
he asked the company.

94
00:03:15,802 --> 00:03:18,231
A witness said the crew answered violently:

95
00:03:18,589 --> 00:03:20,054
no, they would shoot him or

96
00:03:20,054 --> 00:03:22,291
whip him to death at the mast first.

97
00:03:22,617 --> 00:03:24,583
Another witness said they

98
00:03:24,583 --> 00:03:27,814
refused "by reason he was a carpenter."

99
00:03:27,814 --> 00:03:29,544
So Davis stayed.

100
00:03:30,239 --> 00:03:31,649
In April seventeen seventeen,

101
00:03:31,649 --> 00:03:33,673
Bellamy's flagship,

102
00:03:33,673 --> 00:03:36,238
the Whydah, was wrecked off Cape Cod.

103
00:03:36,238 --> 00:03:38,040
Davis was one of only two men

104
00:03:38,040 --> 00:03:40,106
known to have survived that wreck.

105
00:03:40,106 --> 00:03:41,934
Even before the wreck,

106
00:03:41,934 --> 00:03:44,072
being wanted did not make him trusted.

107
00:03:44,072 --> 00:03:45,834
A shipmate testified in

108
00:03:45,834 --> 00:03:47,252
Boston that he had kept his

109
00:03:47,252 --> 00:03:49,347
distance from Davis aboard the Whydah.

110
00:03:49,347 --> 00:03:51,386
The pirates suspected Davis because

111
00:03:51,386 --> 00:03:53,025
he was a forced man,

112
00:03:53,025 --> 00:03:55,498
and the shipmate did not want them to think the two of

113
00:03:55,498 --> 00:03:58,049
them were planning to run away together.

114
00:03:58,049 --> 00:03:59,989
Blackbeard's crew did the same thing

115
00:03:59,989 --> 00:04:02,588
with La Concorde, the slave ship they

116
00:04:02,588 --> 00:04:04,719
captured in November seventeen seventeen.

117
00:04:05,367 --> 00:04:06,772
The Queen Anne's Revenge

118
00:04:06,772 --> 00:04:08,738
project lists carpenters among the

119
00:04:08,738 --> 00:04:10,898
ten Frenchmen forced to stay.

120
00:04:10,898 --> 00:04:12,683
Johnson's book alone tells of

121
00:04:12,683 --> 00:04:14,547
Edward Low's crew forcing away

122
00:04:14,547 --> 00:04:16,378
the carpenter of a captured ship.

123
00:04:16,378 --> 00:04:19,149
A few weeks ago, when your crew took the second sloop,

124
00:04:19,149 --> 00:04:19,924
the faster one,

125
00:04:19,924 --> 00:04:22,738
somebody suggested keeping her carpenter as your mate.

126
00:04:22,738 --> 00:04:24,372
You looked at a frightened man

127
00:04:24,372 --> 00:04:26,537
holding his tool bag with both hands.

128
00:04:26,537 --> 00:04:29,096
You argued that a forced man with a

129
00:04:29,096 --> 00:04:30,902
saw is not a man you want behind you.

130
00:04:30,902 --> 00:04:32,343
The vote went your way.

131
00:04:32,796 --> 00:04:34,203
You still feel good about that,

132
00:04:34,203 --> 00:04:36,591
and slightly less good about why you said it.

133
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:38,337
Then a sail appears,

134
00:04:38,337 --> 00:04:41,760
the chase begins, and the merchant ship surrenders.

135
00:04:41,760 --> 00:04:43,820
When the boarders climb aboard,

136
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:45,535
you go with them.

137
00:04:45,535 --> 00:04:46,936
Not for the money.

138
00:04:46,936 --> 00:04:48,042
For the nails.

139
00:04:48,042 --> 00:04:49,921
On Roberts's ships,

140
00:04:49,921 --> 00:04:51,483
this was a known routine.

141
00:04:51,483 --> 00:04:54,573
At the Cape Coast Castle trial in seventeen twenty-two,

142
00:04:54,573 --> 00:04:55,383
a witness

143
00:04:55,383 --> 00:04:58,246
said one carpenter's mate went

144
00:04:58,246 --> 00:05:00,474
aboard prizes to fetch carpenter's stores,

145
00:05:00,474 --> 00:05:02,177
"as was the custom among them."

146
00:05:02,177 --> 00:05:03,960
The mate of the King Solomon

147
00:05:03,960 --> 00:05:06,742
remembered that same man coming over in the

148
00:05:06,742 --> 00:05:08,541
pirates' boat for carpenter's stores,

149
00:05:08,541 --> 00:05:10,675
and threatening the ship's own carpenter

150
00:05:10,675 --> 00:05:13,337
for bringing up only one bucket of rosin.

151
00:05:13,337 --> 00:05:15,492
Johnson's version of the record lists

152
00:05:15,492 --> 00:05:17,030
the robbery job by job:

153
00:05:17,030 --> 00:05:21,229
the boatswain took the cables, the cooper the casks,

154
00:05:21,229 --> 00:05:23,686
and this man "the carpenter's stores."

155
00:05:23,686 --> 00:05:25,255
His name was John Griffin,

156
00:05:25,255 --> 00:05:27,968
a ship's carpenter from Blackwall, near London.

157
00:05:28,406 --> 00:05:30,027
A witness said it was common talk

158
00:05:30,027 --> 00:05:32,782
aboard that all the men taken from his ship,

159
00:05:32,782 --> 00:05:34,631
the Mercy, had been forced.

160
00:05:34,631 --> 00:05:35,997
It did not save him.

161
00:05:35,997 --> 00:05:37,346
Griffin was condemned,

162
00:05:37,346 --> 00:05:40,094
and then became one of the twenty men whose

163
00:05:40,094 --> 00:05:41,884
sentence was changed to seven years of

164
00:05:41,884 --> 00:05:44,446
forced labour on the West African coast.

165
00:05:44,446 --> 00:05:46,013
Being valuable to pirates did

166
00:05:46,013 --> 00:05:48,036
not make a man safe from the court.

167
00:05:48,036 --> 00:05:50,636
A shipmate said another carpenter,

168
00:05:50,636 --> 00:05:52,405
taken from a different ship,

169
00:05:52,405 --> 00:05:56,126
had been forced, then "voted carpenter" by the pirates,

170
00:05:56,126 --> 00:05:57,481
a post he accepted "very

171
00:05:57,481 --> 00:06:00,961
unwillingly," often lamenting his condition.

172
00:06:00,961 --> 00:06:03,393
Another witness said Roberts used

173
00:06:03,393 --> 00:06:05,838
oaths and foul language to send him

174
00:06:05,838 --> 00:06:08,813
aboard prizes for whatever stores they wanted,

175
00:06:08,813 --> 00:06:10,853
"saying he was the best judge."

176
00:06:11,627 --> 00:06:12,936
That carpenter told the

177
00:06:12,936 --> 00:06:14,846
court about a captured brigantine.

178
00:06:14,846 --> 00:06:15,924
Her own carpenter

179
00:06:15,924 --> 00:06:17,511
warned him she was leaky.

180
00:06:17,511 --> 00:06:19,573
So he went back to Roberts

181
00:06:19,573 --> 00:06:21,085
to object to taking her pump.

182
00:06:21,085 --> 00:06:22,496
Roberts swore that he would

183
00:06:22,496 --> 00:06:23,949
have not only the pump,

184
00:06:23,949 --> 00:06:26,418
but the mainmast too if he wanted it.

185
00:06:26,418 --> 00:06:28,292
A second witness said the carpenter

186
00:06:28,292 --> 00:06:30,290
had gone to Roberts twice about it.

187
00:06:30,632 --> 00:06:32,271
Robbing a leaking ship of her

188
00:06:32,271 --> 00:06:34,074
pump is about the cruellest thing

189
00:06:34,074 --> 00:06:35,905
one carpenter can do to another.

190
00:06:35,905 --> 00:06:38,419
You think about that while you choose.

191
00:06:38,419 --> 00:06:40,766
Oakum, for caulking seams.

192
00:06:40,766 --> 00:06:41,200
Tar.

193
00:06:41,200 --> 00:06:41,809
Nails.

194
00:06:41,809 --> 00:06:43,103
A coil of line.

195
00:06:43,103 --> 00:06:46,184
One good spare spar that will not fit anywhere,

196
00:06:46,184 --> 00:06:47,845
and that you take anyway.

197
00:06:47,845 --> 00:06:49,994
The merchant ship's own carpenter

198
00:06:49,994 --> 00:06:52,088
watches you from beside her mainmast.

199
00:06:52,088 --> 00:06:53,411
He is younger than you,

200
00:06:53,411 --> 00:06:56,391
with a tool bag he clearly paid for himself.

201
00:06:56,391 --> 00:06:58,207
He does not say a word.

202
00:06:58,207 --> 00:07:00,003
He does not have to.

203
00:07:00,003 --> 00:07:02,011
You both know exactly what

204
00:07:02,011 --> 00:07:03,450
a missing auger means three

205
00:07:03,450 --> 00:07:05,764
weeks from the nearest harbor.

206
00:07:05,764 --> 00:07:07,126
You leave him his tools.

207
00:07:07,126 --> 00:07:09,276
You leave the merchant his pump.

208
00:07:09,276 --> 00:07:10,822
Neither of them thanks you.

209
00:07:10,822 --> 00:07:12,714
From their side of the rail,

210
00:07:12,714 --> 00:07:14,713
a polite robber is still a robber.

211
00:07:14,713 --> 00:07:15,798
One eyewitness did

212
00:07:15,798 --> 00:07:19,122
remember a decent pirate carpenter.

213
00:07:19,122 --> 00:07:20,284
In seventeen nineteen,

214
00:07:20,649 --> 00:07:23,552
William Snelgrave, the slave-trading captain held by

215
00:07:23,552 --> 00:07:25,081
pirates in West Africa,

216
00:07:25,081 --> 00:07:29,009
found a pirate carpenter asleep on his own bed in his cabin.

217
00:07:29,009 --> 00:07:30,881
When the carpenter woke,

218
00:07:30,881 --> 00:07:32,036
Snelgrave wrote,

219
00:07:32,036 --> 00:07:35,583
he politely told him to go and get some rest.

220
00:07:35,583 --> 00:07:36,677
Later that night,

221
00:07:36,677 --> 00:07:39,994
a drunk boatswain came in and abused Snelgrave,

222
00:07:39,994 --> 00:07:41,373
and the carpenter called the

223
00:07:41,373 --> 00:07:43,764
boatswain a base villain and threw him out.

224
00:07:44,186 --> 00:07:46,610
When a gust of wind blew their candle out,

225
00:07:46,610 --> 00:07:47,850
the boatswain accused

226
00:07:47,850 --> 00:07:49,646
Snelgrave of trying to sneak into

227
00:07:49,646 --> 00:07:51,967
the powder room and blow up the ship.

228
00:07:51,967 --> 00:07:53,980
The carpenter told him it was an accident,

229
00:07:53,980 --> 00:07:54,411
and that

230
00:07:54,411 --> 00:07:56,672
Snelgrave was still sitting beside him.

231
00:07:56,672 --> 00:07:58,187
In Snelgrave's story,

232
00:07:58,187 --> 00:08:01,239
the most reasonable man aboard a pirate

233
00:08:01,239 --> 00:08:02,995
ship was the one with the saw.

234
00:08:02,995 --> 00:08:05,410
The second sloop is now yours to fix.

235
00:08:05,410 --> 00:08:07,654
Taking a ship was one thing.

236
00:08:07,654 --> 00:08:10,510
Turning her into a pirate ship was another,

237
00:08:10,510 --> 00:08:12,193
and that was carpenters' work.

238
00:08:12,584 --> 00:08:14,344
Real crews did it constantly.

239
00:08:14,344 --> 00:08:16,431
Most of La Concorde's cannons had

240
00:08:16,431 --> 00:08:17,642
been left in France,

241
00:08:17,642 --> 00:08:20,880
the Smithsonian notes, to make room for her captives.

242
00:08:20,880 --> 00:08:23,013
Yet Royal Museums Greenwich says

243
00:08:23,013 --> 00:08:25,368
Blackbeard equipped her with an

244
00:08:25,368 --> 00:08:27,255
imposing forty guns as his flagship.

245
00:08:27,255 --> 00:08:29,820
Someone had to make room for those guns,

246
00:08:29,820 --> 00:08:30,721
fit them and keep

247
00:08:30,721 --> 00:08:32,729
that ship sound under their weight.

248
00:08:33,134 --> 00:08:35,768
Ships do not arm themselves.

249
00:08:35,768 --> 00:08:37,958
At Whydah in seventeen twenty-two,

250
00:08:37,958 --> 00:08:40,164
as Captain Chaloner Ogle reported,

251
00:08:40,164 --> 00:08:43,013
Roberts's men carried off a French ship

252
00:08:43,013 --> 00:08:45,193
planning to fit her for their own service.

253
00:08:45,193 --> 00:08:46,354
Every plan like that

254
00:08:46,354 --> 00:08:48,269
ended at a carpenter's bench.

255
00:08:48,269 --> 00:08:49,801
Your bench is the deck.

256
00:08:49,801 --> 00:08:52,326
You open up space below for more men,

257
00:08:52,326 --> 00:08:54,622
mend the rails and patch the boat.

258
00:08:54,622 --> 00:08:57,630
The crew calls this "making her ready."

259
00:08:57,630 --> 00:09:00,082
You call it three weeks of splinters.

260
00:09:00,082 --> 00:09:03,260
Then a squall hits both sloops at night.

261
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:03,981
By morning,

262
00:09:03,981 --> 00:09:07,205
the old sloop's mast has a long crack near the top.

263
00:09:07,205 --> 00:09:09,675
Nobody can buy a new mast at sea.

264
00:09:10,096 --> 00:09:11,549
So you bind the cracked mast

265
00:09:11,549 --> 00:09:13,864
between two lengths of spare timber,

266
00:09:13,864 --> 00:09:14,895
lashed tight with rope,

267
00:09:14,895 --> 00:09:17,163
the way you were taught as an apprentice.

268
00:09:17,633 --> 00:09:19,247
That spare spar you stole is

269
00:09:19,247 --> 00:09:22,107
suddenly the most popular object aboard.

270
00:09:22,107 --> 00:09:24,309
The captain asks whether it will hold.

271
00:09:24,309 --> 00:09:24,987
You say yes,

272
00:09:24,987 --> 00:09:28,467
in the voice carpenters use when they mean "probably".

273
00:09:28,467 --> 00:09:30,155
The crew hears "yes".

274
00:09:30,706 --> 00:09:32,277
The mast hears "probably".

275
00:09:32,277 --> 00:09:34,840
You spend the next week looking up.

276
00:09:35,278 --> 00:09:36,015
In a chase,

277
00:09:36,015 --> 00:09:38,289
your place is not with the boarders.

278
00:09:38,289 --> 00:09:40,766
You stand below with wooden plugs,

279
00:09:40,766 --> 00:09:42,276
oakum and a mallet,

280
00:09:42,276 --> 00:09:45,886
ready for the moment a shot comes through the side.

281
00:09:45,886 --> 00:09:48,246
On this voyage, it never does.

282
00:09:48,246 --> 00:09:50,790
Nobody thanks you for that either.

283
00:09:50,790 --> 00:09:52,105
That is the job:

284
00:09:52,105 --> 00:09:55,365
when you do it well, nothing happens.

285
00:09:55,804 --> 00:09:57,302
Then comes careening.

286
00:09:57,302 --> 00:09:59,914
As our earlier episodes showed,

287
00:09:59,914 --> 00:10:02,509
crews had to haul a ship onto her side on a

288
00:10:02,509 --> 00:10:04,683
hidden beach and scrape away weed,

289
00:10:04,683 --> 00:10:08,379
barnacles and the worms that bore into the wood.

290
00:10:08,379 --> 00:10:09,865
Everyone scrapes.

291
00:10:09,865 --> 00:10:11,625
Only you decide which

292
00:10:11,625 --> 00:10:12,932
planks are still sound.

293
00:10:12,932 --> 00:10:14,347
When you find a soft one,

294
00:10:14,347 --> 00:10:16,286
the whole crew looks at you as if

295
00:10:16,286 --> 00:10:18,181
you put the worm there yourself.

296
00:10:18,181 --> 00:10:19,908
Being the man with the

297
00:10:19,908 --> 00:10:21,062
saw has a darker side.

298
00:10:21,062 --> 00:10:23,019
If there is no surgeon aboard,

299
00:10:23,019 --> 00:10:25,000
the crew starts looking at you

300
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:27,044
whenever someone is badly hurt.

301
00:10:27,044 --> 00:10:27,903
In seventeen twenty-four,

302
00:10:28,228 --> 00:10:31,594
a Boston court heard that one of John Phillips's pirates,

303
00:10:31,594 --> 00:10:33,853
William Phillips, had lost a leg.

304
00:10:33,853 --> 00:10:35,361
Johnson's book alone

305
00:10:35,361 --> 00:10:36,675
claims who took it off.

306
00:10:36,675 --> 00:10:38,486
There was no surgeon aboard,

307
00:10:38,486 --> 00:10:39,475
so the crew chose the

308
00:10:39,475 --> 00:10:41,303
carpenter as "the most proper man."

309
00:10:41,852 --> 00:10:43,442
Johnson says the carpenter did it

310
00:10:43,442 --> 00:10:44,798
with the tools of his trade,

311
00:10:44,798 --> 00:10:47,640
and that, against the odds, the man survived.

312
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:49,990
The court records add a strange detail.

313
00:10:49,990 --> 00:10:51,984
When a captured fisherman offered to

314
00:10:51,984 --> 00:10:54,572
take William Phillips home, he refused.

315
00:10:54,572 --> 00:10:57,492
If he went, he said, they would hang him.

316
00:10:57,492 --> 00:10:59,170
Captain John Phillips himself,

317
00:10:59,170 --> 00:11:01,986
Johnson says, had been bred a carpenter.

318
00:11:01,986 --> 00:11:04,162
Pirates had taken him from a

319
00:11:04,162 --> 00:11:05,617
ship bound for Newfoundland,

320
00:11:05,617 --> 00:11:07,423
and he soon took to the life.

321
00:11:07,813 --> 00:11:10,306
A Newfoundland history article agrees:

322
00:11:10,306 --> 00:11:13,166
Phillips was a ship's carpenter by trade.

323
00:11:13,166 --> 00:11:14,464
On your sloop, at least,

324
00:11:14,464 --> 00:11:17,694
the surgeon is right there, still in his leather apron.

325
00:11:17,694 --> 00:11:19,385
You consider him the best

326
00:11:19,385 --> 00:11:21,217
thing this crew ever stole.

327
00:11:21,217 --> 00:11:23,242
Carpenters were valued,

328
00:11:23,242 --> 00:11:25,695
but valued is not the same as free.

329
00:11:26,423 --> 00:11:28,152
Only Johnson's book prints the

330
00:11:28,152 --> 00:11:30,574
articles of John Phillips's crew.

331
00:11:30,574 --> 00:11:31,893
They gave the master,

332
00:11:31,893 --> 00:11:35,007
the carpenter, the boatswain and the gunner one

333
00:11:35,007 --> 00:11:37,093
share and a quarter each,

334
00:11:37,093 --> 00:11:39,937
almost as much as the captain's one and a half.

335
00:11:39,937 --> 00:11:42,078
The same articles said that any man

336
00:11:42,078 --> 00:11:44,751
who tried to run away would be marooned.

337
00:11:45,108 --> 00:11:47,437
Johnson says Phillips's carpenter,

338
00:11:47,437 --> 00:11:48,635
a man named Fern,

339
00:11:48,635 --> 00:11:51,820
was offended when the new arrival John Rose Archer was

340
00:11:51,820 --> 00:11:54,449
made quartermaster over the older hands.

341
00:11:54,449 --> 00:11:55,577
You may remember Archer

342
00:11:55,577 --> 00:11:57,611
from the quartermaster episode.

343
00:11:57,611 --> 00:11:59,058
Fern tried to sail off with

344
00:11:59,058 --> 00:12:00,908
a captured prize, and failed.

345
00:12:01,377 --> 00:12:02,577
Later he tried again,

346
00:12:02,577 --> 00:12:05,175
and Johnson says Phillips killed him for it,

347
00:12:05,175 --> 00:12:06,992
according to their articles.

348
00:12:06,992 --> 00:12:09,145
A deposition in the Boston court

349
00:12:09,145 --> 00:12:11,179
files confirms that a man named

350
00:12:11,179 --> 00:12:13,810
Ferne had been shot by Captain Phillips.

351
00:12:13,810 --> 00:12:16,194
Phillips needed a new carpenter.

352
00:12:16,775 --> 00:12:18,133
In March seventeen twenty-four,

353
00:12:18,133 --> 00:12:21,816
his crew took one from a ship bound from

354
00:12:21,816 --> 00:12:24,301
Virginia to London: Edward Cheeseman.

355
00:12:24,850 --> 00:12:26,450
The indictment in Boston says

356
00:12:26,450 --> 00:12:28,723
plainly that the pirates forced him.

357
00:12:29,113 --> 00:12:30,890
Johnson calls Cheeseman "a

358
00:12:30,890 --> 00:12:32,898
modest sober young man,

359
00:12:33,353 --> 00:12:36,046
very averse to their unlawful practice."

360
00:12:36,983 --> 00:12:38,386
The man ordered to row him

361
00:12:38,386 --> 00:12:41,063
to the pirate ship was a captured fisherman,

362
00:12:41,063 --> 00:12:42,203
John Fillmore.

363
00:12:42,203 --> 00:12:44,757
Johnson says Fillmore saw how

364
00:12:44,757 --> 00:12:46,266
unwilling Cheeseman was,

365
00:12:46,266 --> 00:12:50,506
and offered to help him overthrow the pirates.

366
00:12:50,506 --> 00:12:53,347
In April seventeen twenty-four, they did.

367
00:12:53,347 --> 00:12:54,897
The captives rose up.

368
00:12:54,897 --> 00:12:57,291
According to the Boston News-Letter,

369
00:12:57,291 --> 00:12:58,995
Cheeseman threw the pirates'

370
00:12:58,995 --> 00:13:00,561
sailing master overboard,

371
00:13:00,561 --> 00:13:03,507
and the crew was overpowered within minutes.

372
00:13:03,507 --> 00:13:04,191
In May,

373
00:13:04,191 --> 00:13:07,992
Cheeseman and Fillmore stood trial in Boston.

374
00:13:07,992 --> 00:13:10,427
The court found them not guilty.

375
00:13:10,427 --> 00:13:12,208
The carpenter the pirates

376
00:13:12,208 --> 00:13:14,052
had forced aboard was the

377
00:13:14,052 --> 00:13:16,100
carpenter who ended their voyage.

378
00:13:16,100 --> 00:13:17,502
History has a sense of

379
00:13:17,502 --> 00:13:19,404
humor about Fillmore, too.

380
00:13:19,404 --> 00:13:20,792
The historian J.

381
00:13:20,792 --> 00:13:21,816
Franklin Jameson,

382
00:13:21,816 --> 00:13:24,527
who published the Boston court records,

383
00:13:24,885 --> 00:13:26,800
notes that John Fillmore was the

384
00:13:26,800 --> 00:13:29,110
great-grandfather of Millard Fillmore,

385
00:13:29,110 --> 00:13:31,914
a future President of the United States.

386
00:13:32,593 --> 00:13:34,279
The fisherman who rowed the

387
00:13:34,279 --> 00:13:37,129
pirates' new carpenter across ended up

388
00:13:37,129 --> 00:13:39,258
in the family tree of a president.

389
00:13:39,258 --> 00:13:40,558
Thomas Davis,

390
00:13:40,558 --> 00:13:43,691
the Whydah survivor, had been tried in Boston too,

391
00:13:43,691 --> 00:13:45,333
in October seventeen seventeen.

392
00:13:45,333 --> 00:13:47,672
He told the court he had asked

393
00:13:47,672 --> 00:13:50,144
again and again to be released.

394
00:13:50,144 --> 00:13:51,827
He was found not guilty.

395
00:13:52,202 --> 00:13:52,831
That is the

396
00:13:52,831 --> 00:13:55,121
uncomfortable thing about your story.

397
00:13:55,641 --> 00:13:57,789
The courts were kindest to carpenters who

398
00:13:57,789 --> 00:14:00,626
could show they never wanted to be there.

399
00:14:00,626 --> 00:14:01,941
You came willingly.

400
00:14:01,941 --> 00:14:03,449
Nobody has your name

401
00:14:03,449 --> 00:14:05,261
written down as a forced man.

402
00:14:05,261 --> 00:14:07,225
Then the dealer brings the news

403
00:14:07,225 --> 00:14:09,405
you have heard before:

404
00:14:09,405 --> 00:14:12,242
the king's pardon of seventeen seventeen.

405
00:14:12,785 --> 00:14:15,966
Surrender to a governor by the fifth of September,

406
00:14:15,966 --> 00:14:16,673
seventeen eighteen,

407
00:14:16,673 --> 00:14:20,810
and earlier piracies would be forgiven.

408
00:14:20,810 --> 00:14:22,631
You read the reward list,

409
00:14:22,631 --> 00:14:26,671
and for the first time in this series, the job is on it.

410
00:14:27,302 --> 00:14:29,236
As the London Gazette printed it,

411
00:14:29,236 --> 00:14:31,463
a pirate commander was worth one hundred pounds,

412
00:14:32,103 --> 00:14:33,722
and "every lieutenant,

413
00:14:33,722 --> 00:14:38,228
master, boatswain, carpenter, and gunner" forty pounds.

414
00:14:39,168 --> 00:14:41,564
The quartermaster was not on that list.

415
00:14:41,564 --> 00:14:43,632
The surgeon was not on it.

416
00:14:43,632 --> 00:14:44,734
You are.

417
00:14:45,671 --> 00:14:46,672
Forty pounds.

418
00:14:46,672 --> 00:14:49,246
Twice what Tom is worth.

419
00:14:50,086 --> 00:14:51,317
You were hoping for a

420
00:14:51,317 --> 00:14:53,051
different kind of recognition.

421
00:14:53,051 --> 00:14:55,255
The crew votes on the pardon.

422
00:14:55,255 --> 00:14:57,619
You came willingly, so you have a vote.

423
00:14:57,619 --> 00:15:00,149
You raise your hand for the pardon.

424
00:15:01,005 --> 00:15:02,623
You know exactly how much a

425
00:15:02,623 --> 00:15:04,955
ship can take before she sinks,

426
00:15:04,955 --> 00:15:07,021
and you think this crew is close.

427
00:15:07,021 --> 00:15:09,276
The pardon wins, narrowly.

428
00:15:09,276 --> 00:15:11,134
Before you go ashore,

429
00:15:11,605 --> 00:15:13,652
you walk the hold one last time and

430
00:15:13,652 --> 00:15:15,831
check the seams by lantern light.

431
00:15:15,831 --> 00:15:17,958
Tom asks why you bother.

432
00:15:17,958 --> 00:15:22,145
You tell him that somebody will sail this sloop next,

433
00:15:22,145 --> 00:15:24,661
and the sea does not care who.

434
00:15:24,661 --> 00:15:27,300
Ashore, the waterfront is full of

435
00:15:27,300 --> 00:15:28,780
boats that need mending,

436
00:15:28,780 --> 00:15:30,952
and nobody asks a carpenter too many

437
00:15:30,952 --> 00:15:33,784
questions while he is fixing their hull.

438
00:15:33,784 --> 00:15:35,528
You find work within a day.

439
00:15:35,528 --> 00:15:36,949
The pay is worse.

440
00:15:37,323 --> 00:15:38,607
Nobody threatens to

441
00:15:38,607 --> 00:15:40,596
shoot you if you ask to leave.

442
00:15:40,596 --> 00:15:42,658
You decide that is a fair trade.

443
00:15:42,658 --> 00:15:44,282
Not everyone made it.

444
00:15:44,928 --> 00:15:46,531
Johnson writes that the greatest

445
00:15:46,531 --> 00:15:48,280
part of the pirates who surrendered

446
00:15:48,280 --> 00:15:49,940
soon returned to the trade,

447
00:15:49,940 --> 00:15:52,739
and the historian who edited Woodes Rogers's

448
00:15:52,739 --> 00:15:55,401
voyage agrees that many resumed it.

449
00:15:55,759 --> 00:15:58,297
A carpenter can always find a ship.

450
00:15:58,297 --> 00:15:59,828
Choosing which one

451
00:15:59,828 --> 00:16:01,762
was always the hard part.

452
00:16:01,762 --> 00:16:03,882
Tom comes by now and then

453
00:16:03,882 --> 00:16:05,708
with a bucket, out of habit.

454
00:16:05,708 --> 00:16:07,163
You let him bail out

455
00:16:07,163 --> 00:16:09,022
whatever you are working on.

456
00:16:09,022 --> 00:16:12,047
Some partnerships are bigger than piracy.

457
00:16:12,047 --> 00:16:13,495
So why was being a pirate

458
00:16:13,495 --> 00:16:15,355
carpenter worse than you think?

459
00:16:15,355 --> 00:16:17,031
Because the crew needed you

460
00:16:17,031 --> 00:16:19,255
too much to ever let you go.

461
00:16:19,255 --> 00:16:21,008
Because you were the plumber,

462
00:16:21,008 --> 00:16:23,558
the shipwright and sometimes the surgeon.

463
00:16:23,558 --> 00:16:25,857
Because you robbed other carpenters of

464
00:16:25,857 --> 00:16:27,998
the tools that kept their ships afloat.

465
00:16:27,998 --> 00:16:29,587
And because a court would ask

466
00:16:29,587 --> 00:16:31,937
the same question the crew never did:

467
00:16:31,937 --> 00:16:33,778
did you come willingly?

468
00:16:34,121 --> 00:16:35,582
Some carpenters who could

469
00:16:35,582 --> 00:16:37,793
prove they were forced went home.

470
00:16:38,119 --> 00:16:39,790
The ones who came willingly

471
00:16:39,790 --> 00:16:42,000
had a price on the king's list.

472
00:16:42,310 --> 00:16:44,820
If you want another life behind the legends,

473
00:16:44,820 --> 00:16:46,527
and another job that

474
00:16:46,527 --> 00:16:49,097
sounds better than it was, subscribe.

475
00:16:49,519 --> 00:16:51,512
This is Pirate Pages.

476
00:16:51,512 --> 00:16:54,062
The lives behind the legends.
